      "text": "To determine the measure of \\(\\angle PQR\\), we need to understand the geometric relationships and the angles involved in the given diagram. Here is the step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. **Identify the known angles and relationships:**\n   - \\(RSP\\) is a straight line, which means \\(\\angle RSP = 180^\\circ\\).\n   - \\(\\angle QSP = 80^\\circ\\).\n   - Since \\(RSP\\) is a straight line, the remaining angle on the same line as \\(\\angle QSP\\) and \\(\\angle RSP\\) will be \\(180^\\circ - 80^\\circ = 100^\\circ\\). This angle is \\(\\angle PSR\\).\n\n2. **Determine \\(\\angle PSR\\):**\n   - Since \\(RSP\\) is a straight line, \\(\\angle PSR\\) is complementary to \\(\\angle QSP\\) with respect to the straight line.\n   - This means that \\(\\angle PSR\\) is the supplementary angle to \\(\\angle QSP\\).\n\n3. **Calculate \\(\\angle PQR\\):**\n   - The angle \\(\\angle PQR\\) is the remaining angle in the straight line triangles \\(PRS\\) and \\(QRS\\).\n   - Since \\(\\angle PSR = 100^\\circ\\), and \\(\\angle PQR\\) is part of this straight line, we can use the fact that the sum of angles around a point or in a straight line is \\(180^\\circ\\).\n\n4. **Use the properties of parallel lines and corresponding angles:**\n   - If we assume that there is a parallel line or a transversal intersecting the line \\(RSP\\) and creating corresponding angles, we can find the measure of \\(\\angle PQR\\).\n\n5. **Confirm the measure of \\(\\angle PQR\\):**\n   - Given that \\(\\angle QSP = 80^\\circ\\), and knowing that the angles around a straight line sum up to \\(180^\\circ\\), the remaining angle \\(\\angle PQR\\) would be:\n   \\[\n   \\angle PQR = 180^\\circ - 80^\\circ - 80^\\circ = 20^\\circ\n   \\]\n\nThus, the measure of \\(\\angle PQR\\) is \\(\\boxed{20}\\) degrees.",
